INT. STARLITE ROLLER RINK - SIX A.M.

Carpet-walled, smelling of floor wax and old nacho cheese. A disco ball turns without light, a silent planet. The maple floor is empty and gleaming like a frozen lake.

DEZI ALVARADO, 15, sits on the bench in secondhand skates, laces undone. Hoodie sleeves swallow her hands. She has been crying and has recently stopped.

A figure leans against the snack counter. A BOY in a faded letterman jacket, tall, thin as a coat rack, a toothpick rolling in his mouth. He watches her without moving.

BOY
You've got two left feet.

DEZI
I've got one left foot and one broken heart.

BOY
Which is worse?

DEZI
Depends who you ask.

She stands, wobbles, grabs the rail. He doesn't help. A kindness, of a kind.

BOY (CONT'D)
They're saying you took your mother's car.

DEZI
Borrowed.

BOY
Without asking.

DEZI
She was asleep.

BOY
Dezi.

She looks at him sharply. He shrugs, almost apologetic.

BOY (CONT'D)
My name's Junior, by the way. Since you're not going to ask.

JUNIOR (CONT'D)
Everyone calls me Junior. My dad's Junior too. Nobody knows who the first one was.

DEZI
That's the saddest thing I've heard all week.

JUNIOR
It's been a long week?

DEZI
Rough.

She pushes off the rail. Rolls two feet. Wavers. Falls. Hard. Sits on the floor with her palms flat.

For a moment, she isn't sure if she's going to scream or laugh.

She does both.

The sound ricochets off the carpeted walls. Junior flinches, then, slowly, grins. He steps onto the floor in his sneakers and slides to her.

JUNIOR (CONT'D)
That's it. Get it out.

DEZI
I hate everyone.

JUNIOR
Me too.

DEZI
I hate my mother.

JUNIOR
Mine's all right. But I hate my dad.

DEZI
I don't hate her. I just wanted to drive somewhere nobody knew.

JUNIOR
And you ended up at a roller rink.

DEZI
It was the only thing open.

The lights come on. All at once, bright as an operating room. The disco ball catches a beam and sprays confetti across the walls.

MS. FAIRWEATHER, 60s, rink manager, a cardigan embroidered with tiny skates, comes through the swinging doors with a ring of keys and a coffee thermos. She stops dead.

MS. FAIRWEATHER
We don't open for four hours.

JUNIOR
Morning, Ms. Fairweather.

MS. FAIRWEATHER
Junior. You are not supposed to be here. Neither is she. Whoever she is.

DEZI
Dezi.

MS. FAIRWEATHER
Dezi. Well. Are you hurt?

DEZI
No.

MS. FAIRWEATHER
Good. Skates off. Both of you. There's a pot of coffee in the back and a patched-up phone. Call your mother.

Dezi looks at Junior. He lifts one shoulder. Almost a smile.

She begins to unlace.
